{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_gate.md collab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-of-record.md'","aggregated_output":"# Slice 9 kernel differential gate: execution evidence correction\n\n## Attribution section — version 1 (attribution-as-query ruling of record)\n\nThis section versions the executed-arity attribution contract per the signed\nruling `1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-of-record.md`\n(v4) and its implementation round. The gate's credit rule is now a query\nover caller-site-tagged execution records, not name+arity string matching.\n\n- **Records.** Every instrumented arity body appends\n  `[guest-function arity caller-site]` to the fixture's guest stream at body\n  entry. The caller site id is assigned at APPLICATION EXPRESSIONS in the\n  compiled AST, post-macroexpansion (the corpus runs under macros; source\n  tagging would miss expanded sites), by a harness-owned instrumentation\n  pass (`kernel-stamp-sites`) that threads each stamped call's argument\n  vector through a guest site cell so the callee's first body form reads\n  the applying site. This is instantiation #1 of the site contract,\n  harness-level per Addendum A; yin.vm and yang.clojure are untouched. The\n  walker/semantic ingresses ingest the stamped program unchanged.\n- **Site tables.** Mechanically derived from each fixture's compiled AST\n  (`R7`): every stamped site's callee, per-argument shapes\n  (`:ambient-literal`, `:literal`, `:variable`, `:computed`,\n  `:dynamic-callee`), enclosing definition, and self-delegation mark —\n  recorded in the versioned [`slice9_site_tables.edn`](slice9_site_tables.edn)\n  (`:version 1`) with the per-fixture canonical-source-hash of the inlined\n  adaptation (R2b case 3's referent: a trace read against a differing hash\n  refuses).\n- **The query.** An upstream function-arity is credited when both C lanes\n  record (a) the arity-preserving guest definition's body entry at the same\n  arity through any real site (the degenerate 1:1 path), or (b) a body\n  entry of a derived correspondence carrier — the guest/context callable\n  and arity the derived correspondence names (`:attribution\n  :ambient-correspondence` in the evidence) — entered through a\n  SITE-LINKED record: the site table's static callee at that site is the\n  carrier itself, and the site is not a self-delegation. The derived\n  correspondence has three kinds. An `:ambient-delegation` path (R3's\n  disjointness pair, `registered-definition?` /1) additionally requires\n  the site's compiled context argument to have resolved to the\n  adapter-supplied ambient literal. `:context-shift` paths — the retired\n  offset table's scope (R6): the kernel-first context callable that\n  replaced the upstream env parameter at the same arity or was added at\n  one fewer argument (unregister-unparser!/1, unparse/1,\n  register-unparser!/2 and /3, install-unfold-implicit!/1,\n  install-fundamental-unparsers!/0, install-opacity-function/1,\n  register-definition /2 and /3, local-definitions /1, …) — and\n  `:context-copy` paths (the verbatim integration copies is-opaque?,\n  unparse-term-once/rec) thread the adapted state explicitly, so their\n  witness is the site-linked body entry of the carrier, whatever context\n  value the site threads; the ambient literal never borrows across kinds.\n  The retired offset table `kernel-context-extra-arguments` (six\n  hand-declared +1 shifts) is replaced by that derived correspondence;\n  its full coverage list is recorded in\n  [`attribution_inventory.md`](attribution_inventory.md).\n- **Refusals.** The seven-case battery\n  (`test-attribution-battery`) pins: explicit `/2` supplying an empty\n  environment never credits `/1` (disjointness by site identity and\n  compiled argument shape, never value equality); unexecuted ambient sites\n  borrow nothing; mismatched canonical-source-hash refuses; a missing lane\n  refuses; a site declared ambient whose compiled argument is not the\n  ambient literal refuses (compile-time AST verification); indirect-origin\n  entries (`:dynamic` sites, the `:gate-site/none` entry site) refuse;\n  variable context arguments are `/2`-only. A declared site id that never\n  appears in any trace is a RECORDED DIAGNOSTIC\n  (`:attribution :stale-site-diagnostics`), never silent non-credit. The\n  `/3` self-delegation keeps its own unmapped site id: no credit, the\n  originating call is the credited event.\n- **H3-6 prep (the audit's query shape).** The independent audit\n  re-derives the site tables from the same compiled ASTs, re-computes each\n  fixture's canonical-source-hash, and re-runs the credit query over the\n  two raw C-lane traces joined to the tables and the derived ambient\n  correspondence; closure requires the reconstructed credit set to equal\n  the gate's, with every withdrawn or gained witness explained.\n\nThe remainder of this document is the prior gate record, unchanged.\n\n---\n\nThe gate on the hardening line is **blocked**. The architect denied the prior\n165/165 closure claim because membership in a static dependency closure did\nnot prove execution of every arity. That claim is withdrawn. The gate retains\nits final assertion `(= :pass (:gate-status evidence))`; missing execution\nevidence must fail that assertion.\n\nThe 106-fixture corpus has **100 passing agreements, two recorded deviations\n(`elab-after-qed`, `check-after-qed`), two blocked fixtures, and two recorded\nupstream failures.** The four narrowly recorded exception dispositions remain\ndeclared departures, not agreements. No upstream or `src/cljc/` source changes\nare made by H3-0. Admission is unchanged: 155 bindings (146 admitted, 9\nexcluded) and 175 candidate function/arities (165 admitted, 10 excluded).\n\nRecorded executions establish **107/165** admitted arities: 39 directly\nand 68 through named integrations. The other **58**, including `parse/1`,\nremain uncredited. The declared departures are carried by the versioned\n[`slice9_declarations.edn`](slice9_declarations.edn) (provenance `ea18c8e4^`),\nrevalidated on every refresh by the representation-aware classifier: the\nguest `:meta` value may be a pre-adoption raw closure or the admitted §8.10\ndescriptor; structurally invalid descriptors and any payload, message, script\nor call mismatch refuse (descriptor authenticity itself is the compiler's\nadmission witness upstream of the outcome). The corrected counts and remaining arities\nare recorded in [`slice9_evidence.edn`](slice9_evidence.edn) and\n[`slice9_unexercised.edn`](slice9_unexercised.edn). The residual consumers in\n[`fixture_closure.edn`](fixture_closure.edn) retain their concrete expected\noutcomes; they do not establish closure of the full inventory, and their\ncount (20) predates the re-derivation — the live unexercised set is the\n58-name list.\n\n## Consumer evidence\n\n- `mkenv` preserves a nonempty global environment and creates empty locals.\n- `beta-red`, `delta-normalize`, and `delta-normalize-local` have explicit\n  entry fixtures and missing-reference rejections. The local wrapper also\n  exercises explicit local definitions and their precedence over an ambient\n  namespace definition.\n- `install-unfold-implicit!` installs into both isolated upstream atoms on A.\n  B/C return an updated immutable context, used by both the delta wrapper and\n  NBE. Both normalizers read the same installed extension. An installed\n  refusing extension propagates its original error data.\n- `install-opacity-function` starts from a refusing policy, explicitly installs\n  the working policy, then enters the original proof dispatcher through\n  opaque/transparent steps and qed. A duplicate opaque step rejects. A uses\n  an isolated namespace and host-local Var policy; B/C thread immutable\n  definition updates through the explicit context.\n- `install-fundamental-unparsers!` starts from an empty registry, installs the\n  fundamental product unparser, then unparses nested implications. Dependent\n  products and atoms remain unchanged. Direct `prod-impl-unparser` fixtures\n  also verify its `[term false]` refusal result.\n- `exists-kw?`, `term?`, `pair?`, and `vectorn?` include false results.\n  `bound-vars` distinguishes a bound occurrence from entirely free variables.\n  These fixtures preserve upstream behavior, including its shallow term\n  validator and the use of `vector` rather than `vector?` in utility predicates.\n- `proper-type?` enters each arity separately. Unknown variables throw;\n  a well-typed term whose inferred type is not a sort returns false.\n- `show-deftype` checks definition, axiom, theorem, implicit, and unknown\n  labels. It depends only on portable record-kind predicates and returns a\n  string. Host pprint and stdout functions remain excluded.\n- `safe-get` covers successful lookup, missing keys, stored false, and stored\n  nil. The latter three all reject upstream. `vremove` removes every match,\n  preserves a no-match vector, and propagates a predicate's domain exception.\n\n`mkenv`, `appify`, and `bound-vars` have no domain rejection in the pinned\nsource; the fixtures preserve their constructor, empty-input, and empty-set\nresults instead of manufacturing refusals. A refused product rewrite is\nidentified by its explicit modification flag, not mistaken for an exception.\n\nThe pinned local delta wrapper has a narrower portability scope than its\ndocstring suggests: `delta-step` drops `local?` when calling `delta-reduction`\non a reference. Consequently upstream can resolve a global or host namespace\ndefinition on that path. The global-result fixture preserves this behavior;\nthe namespace fixture supplies a local definition that shadows the ambient\none. The guest boundary still requires explicit definitions and has no host\nnamespace lookup capability. This round does not repair upstream or claim\nagreement for ambient-only resolution.\n\n## Acceptance counting\n\nEvery selected guest function body appends `[guest-function arity]` to an\nexplicit diagnostic stream at entry. Instrumentation covers all arities of\nthe functions supplied to each fixture, including higher-order calls through\nregistries and extension closures. Each C lane creates its own stream.\nAfter the VM halts, the harness reads that stream through its own\ncursor, preserving the fixture's original outcome and exception payload.\nA/B remain uninstrumented. Raw ordered call records are retained under\n`:c-walker :executed-calls` and `:c-semantic :executed-calls` for every completed\nfixture. The original uninstrumented fixtures used 1,000,000 instructions per\nlane. The instrumented fixtures have a 2,000,000 instruction bound because\nbody-entry stream writes add guest instructions. Reading the completed\ndiagnostic stream is harness work after halt. This is an instrumentation\nbudget change, not a new performance acceptance claim.\n\nPassing direct fixtures contribute only their original function/argument\ncount, and only with matching body-entry records in both C lanes. Passing\nnamed integrations contribute only exact arities recorded in both C lanes.\nStatic dependency membership contributes no credit. Full branch coverage is\nnot required. Context adapter identities and explicit extra context arguments\nare recorded in the evidence; each observed guest arity is checked against\nthat mapping individually, never against all arities of its function.\n\nOnly `:pass` fixture rows contribute exercise evidence. Recorded upstream\nfailures and deviations do not contribute. Any admitted arity without a\npassing direct or named integration execution path blocks the gate.\n\nThe accounting regression first failed against the old implementation: it\ncredited the deliberately uncalled arity and accepted missing C-lane traces.\nIt now requires both traces and leaves unused arities uncredited. A second\nregression replaces the adapted `parse/1` body in memory with an unconditional\nerror, then runs `:parse-to-normalize-to-type`. A/B and both C lanes still\npass; both C traces record `parse/2` and no `parse/1`, and the counter leaves\n`parse/1` uncredited. Every credited path is also checked against the recorded\nexecutions of its named fixture. See\n[`slice9_arity_accounting_test.txt`](slice9_arity_accounting_test.txt).\n\n## Recorded portability boundaries\n\nThe two `:recorded-deviation` rows remain `:elab-after-qed` and\n`:check-after-qed`. Upstream embeds `clojure.core/meta`, a host function, in\nexception data; C carries a guest closure. The original outcomes, comparison\nflags, exact payload guards, and evidence pointers remain intact. Both rows\nare listed in `:recorded-portability-boundaries` in the summary. Neither is\nreported as agreement.\n\n## Recorded upstream failures\n\n`:typing-ascription` still reaches the upstream declaration\n`type-of-ascribe`, which has no definition. A raises its original unbound-fn\nexception; B/C retain the explicit branch-local refusal.\n\n`:compile-malformed` still passes scalar proof input `[42]`. A/B raise the\noriginal native sequence exception; C retains its portable sequence refusal.\n\nThese two rows have the distinct disposition `:recorded-upstream-failure` and\nare listed in `:recorded-upstream-failures`. Their original A/B/C records and\nfalse agreement flags remain available. The disposition checks the exact\nfixture ID, input call, exception class/message, B outcome, and both completed\nC outcomes. Changed calls, IDs, or C payloads are tested to refuse the\ndisposition. Any other execution failure remains a blocker. No upstream fix,\naccepted-negative claim, or equality projection is introduced for these rows.\n\n## Verification\n\nAn independent audit re-derives the credited arities and fixture paths from\nthe two raw C traces. Kondo reports zero errors and warnings. The full oracle\nruns 26 tests and 1,697 assertions with exactly one failure and no errors:\nits retained `(= :pass (:gate-status evidence))` closure assertion. The\nunexercised arities — **58** under the current accounting, including\n`parse/1` — therefore continue to block acceptance. Verification results are\nrecorded in [`slice9_verification.edn`](slice9_verification.edn), with command\noutputs beside it.\n`yang.latte-oracle-test/write-kernel-gate-evidence!` explicitly regenerates\nboth execution evidence and the remaining-arity inventory; normal tests do\nnot write them. Evidence retains the pinned upstream revision, compiler\ndigest, runtime, instruction budget, per-fixture outcomes, diagnostic traces,\ndependency graph, admission inventory, credited paths, and all four recorded\nexception dispositions.\n\nThe append-only correction in [`known-gaps.md`](../../../docs/agents/known-gaps.md)\nrecords that host Clojure turns a thrown string into `ClassCastException`,\ncaught by `catch Exception` but not `catch ExceptionInfo`. Portable `try` binds\nthe error payload without equivalent runtime class handling; this guest/host\ngap remains declared.\n\nExecution scope is both C evaluators on the JVM. This correction does not\nclaim renewed architect sign-off or Node/Dart verification, and the blocked\ngate does not authorize program closure or merge.\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-05 03:40:00 GMT (v1)\nRevised-GMT: 2026-11-05 18:20:00 GMT (v2 — astra's four findings)\nRevised-GMT: 2026-11-05 20:10:00 GMT (v3 — qwen3.8-max's five amendments)\nRevised-GMT: 2026-11-06 02:45:00 GMT (v4 — kimi k3.1-pro's four amendments; verdict: endorse for owner sign-off with these)\n# RULING OF RECORD — attribution-as-query: caller-side site tagging; record, then attribute by query\n\nStatus: SIGNED by the owner (2026-11-06, \"i sign-off\") after three independent reviews (gpt-6-astra v1: 4 findings; qwen3.8-max v2: 5 amendments; kimi k3.1-pro v3: 4 amendments — all incorporated). This document is now the ruling-of-record and the implementation contract.\n\nReview state: v1 → astra (4 findings); v2 → qwen3.8-max (5 amendments); v3 → kimi k3.1-pro (4 amendments, below). Architecture settled across all three; v4's changes are mechanical corrections to the mechanism and the battery, per kimi: \"(1) caller-side AST tagging; (2) case 5 redefined + a seventh case; (3) the trace-shape-induced revision of ALL pins and the accounting rewrite; (4) R1's trigger and format conditions.\" Central principle (all three accept): derive credit from recorded relationships, require actual body entry in both lanes, refuse ambiguity. Unitarity preserves what flows through the pipeline; it cannot recover provenance never recorded — this ruling records the missing provenance minimally, then attributes by query.\n\n## R1 — The durable correspondence record is DESCOPED, on stated conditions\n\nAs v3: the address-linked adaptation manifest is deferred — the adapter is inlined per fixture, addresses need canonicalization nothing yet consumes, and no second consumer exists. TWO CONDITIONS from kimi's review: (a) **the deferred trigger is an OWNER/ARCHITECT call** — \"a second consumer exists\" is never self-declared by an implementer; (b) **the declared site tables are recorded in a stable, versioned evidence format** so the later R1 landing is an addition to the record, not a migration of it. The long-term stream-citizen direction stands as design intent. The offset table `kernel-context-extra-arguments` is retired — BUT its retirement has mechanical scope (see R6): it serves the context-API arities across the whole family, and its consumers (kernel-executed-arity? and ~30 pinned assertions) are rewritten with it.\n\n## R2 — The mechanism: CALLER-SIDE site tagging in the compiled AST; the callee record carries the caller's site id\n\n**Site ids are assigned at APPLICATION EXPRESSIONS in the compiled AST, post-macroexpansion** — not at callee body entry, and not on raw source forms (the corpus runs under `is`/`testing`/LaTTe macros; source-level tagging would silently miss every macro-expanded call site). The callee-entry trace record is extended to carry the **caller's site id**: the record becomes `[fname arity caller-site-id]`. Rationale (kimi's finding): the ambiguity is one guest callee clause serving two upstream arities — callee-side extension yields identical records for both origins and cannot work. Consequence for the scoping inventory: the **walker/semantic ingress row is updated** — the walker gains site-id stamping at application nodes; it is not \"nothing further.\" Indirect applications (`(:implicit-fn implicit-def)` and kin) have static expression positions, so they receive static site ids like any other call; the callee being runtime-unknown does not affect the id. Recursion is safe (existence-based consumers). The `/3` self-delegation gets its own unmapped site id → no credit — the originating-call rule (R3) is expressible only under this caller-side form, which is why the ruling mandates it.\n\n## R3 — Disjoint attribution patterns, verified by caller-site linkage\n\nA body entry attributes to upstream `/1` only via a site whose compiled argument expression is the ambient literal (`empty-env` supplied by the adapter's declared path); explicit-context sites attribute to `/2`. Disjointness is by recorded site identity and compiled argument shape, never by value equality.\n\n## R2b — The refusal battery: SEVEN cases\n\n1. An explicit `/2` call supplying an empty environment must NOT credit `/1`.\n2. An unexecuted ambient `/1` beside an executed explicit `/2`: no borrowing.\n3. Mismatched program/adaptation evidence: attribution refuses. **The referent is defined**: the mismatch is the canonical-source-hash of the inlined adapter, computed by the compiler during instrumentation and recorded per fixture — a trace from a fixture whose recorded hash differs refuses.\n4. Missing-lane evidence: attribution refuses.\n5. **REDEFINED** (kimi: the v3 case was unimplementable — the tagged trace records no arguments): the fixture's declared call shape is verified against the **compiled AST argument expression at that site** — a site declared ambient whose AST argument is not the ambient literal refuses. The check is compile-time, not value-sniffing.\n6. Indirect-origin: body entries originating through indirect application refuse unless site-linked; default refusal (static ids make indirect sites linkable — they are not exempt).\n7. **NEW (kimi)**: variable/derived argument shapes — a site whose context argument is a variable (bound to `empty-env` on one path, a populated env on another) is statically unclassifiable: **default refusal or `/2`-only attribution**; the declaration is checked against the AST argument expression (an `empty-env` literal may claim ambient; anything else may not).\n8. **NEW (kimi, diagnostic)**: a declared site whose id never appears in any trace (stale table after a fixture edit) is a RECORDED DIAGNOSTIC — a mistyped table must not masquerade as honest refusal.\n\nThe `/3` self-delegation: the delegating call gets its own unmapped site id → no credit; the originating call is the credited event.\n\n## R4 — The attribution contract is versioned where the rule lives\n\nUnchanged from v3: the differential gate record (slice9_gate.md) versions the attribution section; `yin.cic.spec` v1 is untouched; \"mapped\" is defined by the declared site tables + caller-site-tagged records; name+arity matching is the degenerate 1:1 optimization.\n\n## R5 — Closure is MEASURED; the revision scope is HONEST about its blast radius\n\nThe attribution may expose unsupported credit among previously credited arities; valid witnesses preserved, withdrawn witnesses reported, H3-6 blocked until independent reconstruction succeeds. **The revision scope (kimi's correction of v3's under-statement): the trace shape change (2-vector → 3-vector) touches EVERY pin and path set built as 2-vector literals** — the refusal pins (`contains?` assertions on `[registered-definition? 2]`), every `kernel-executed-arity?` path set across the 20-arity family's ~30 assertions, and the gate-status computation (which must learn the third field or the credit set misparses). All of it is authorized by this ruling and listed in the evidence. \"No valid pin regresses\" means: pins encoding current semantics do not *silently* regress; pins requiring mechanical shape updates are updated and listed; pins encoding superseded semantics are revised on the record.\n\n## R6 — The retirement of the offset table is SCOPED to its real consumers\n\n`kernel-context-extra-arguments` serves the context-API arities across the family (not just F2) and is consumed by `kernel-executed-arity?`. Retiring it means **rewriting the accounting predicate** — the declared site tables must cover every context-API arity it served, or the retirement is partial and two mechanisms coexist (the failure qwen's amendment 4 warned against). The implementation round states the coverage list explicitly before deleting the table. The refusal pins' shape updates are covered by R5's authorization. (v3's \"the refusal pins are untouched\" is corrected: they are *mechanically updated* to the 3-vector shape, on the record.)\n\n## R7 — H3-6's audit runs the query — and re-derives the site tables\n\nUnchanged from v3, plus kimi's trust-model hardening: **the declared site tables are DERIVED MECHANICALLY from the compiled AST during instrumentation** (the compiler sees each site's argument expression), and H3-6's independent audit **re-derives them** from the same record — a wrong declaration is a compile/audit failure, not an honor-system risk.\n\n## Scoping inventory (astra + qwen + kimi reviewed)\n\n| Layer | Present now | Missing for the query |\n|---|---|---|\n| Source/adaptation | pinned upstream source, `context.edn`, host assembly + rewrites | declared site tables (mechanically derived; versioned format); retired offset table |\n| Compilation | `kernel-compile` AST per fixture (adapter INLINED; canonical source hash computed) | CALLER-SIDE site-id stamping at application nodes (post-expansion); per-fixture hash recording |\n| Walker/semantic ingress | AST → datoms appended to a program medium | **UPDATED**: application-node site-id stamping in the walker; callee records extended with the caller's site id |\n| Execution evidence | ordered body-entry pairs, both C lanes, outcomes | the third trace field (caller site id); the declared site tables |\n| Trace/budget | `stream/make` 1000000; 2M-bound precedent | restated capacity + instruction bound for the added field |\n| Gate accounting | manifest-derived denominator, name/arity matching; 2-vector trace literals throughout | `dao.space.query/q` attribution + the site tables + the 3-vector migration |\n| Manifest schema | schema-1, CLOSED key set | nothing (linkage evidence is out-of-band; schema-2 only if R1's durable record later lands) |\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- The SEVEN-case battery + the stale-table diagnostic: all refusals refuse; the disjoint patterns attribute correctly on both lanes.\n- The attribution reconstructs the credit set; withdrawn witnesses reported; 165/165 only if every attribution is supported.\n- The pin revisions (mechanical shape updates + the semantic /1 re-credit) listed in the evidence; the `:gate-status` assertion PASSES for the first time; H3-6's independent audit re-derives the site tables and completes closure with final architect sign-off.\n- No valid pin silently regresses; prior rulings stand; the trace capacity restated before any tagged-trace run; R1's deferred trigger reserved to the owner/architect.\n\n## Implementation ordering\n\nFirst deliverable: the written inventory confirmation (the instrumentation touch points, the capacity measurement, the offset-table coverage list). Then: caller-side site-id stamping in the walker/compile → the mechanically derived site tables → the seven-case battery + diagnostic → the accounting rewrite + pin migration → the attribution + re-credit → H3-6.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'wc -l test/yang/latte_oracle/{slice9_site_tables.edn,slice9_evidence.edn,attribution_inventory.md} test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj; cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md; git status --short'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'wc -l test/yang/latte_oracle/{slice9_site_tables.edn,slice9_evidence.edn,attribution_inventory.md} test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj; cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md; git status --short'","aggregated_output":"       2 test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_site_tables.edn\n     191 test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_evidence.edn\n     267 test/yang/latte_oracle/attribution_inventory.md\n    3378 test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj\n    3838 total\n---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# When a test fails, reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the failing test\n# (`clojure -M:test -n <ns> -v <ns>/<var>`, Node `--test=<ns>`, Dart `--only <ns>`) --\n# never the whole suite (owner ruling 2026-11-01; see Gate Ladder G0).\n# Individual tests run fine on the laptop; suite-scale runs (G1 subsystem sets,\n# G2 changed sets, full suites, slow tier, nightly) run on shiva/jupiter --\n# the suite has outgrown the laptop (owner ruling 2026-11-04).\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` runs once daily on the remote hosts (04:00 VN);\n# a MERGE needs only the affected tests (bb test:changed) -- owner ruling 2026-11-01.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Sharded quick JVM feedback and change routing\n\n```sh\nbb test:quick-sharded                           # Full G3-sized JVM quick lane, 4 workers\nbb test:quick-sharded --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\nbb test:quick-sharded --base master --workers 6  # Changed selection, explicit worker limit\nbb test:quick-sharded --list --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\nbb test:routed docs/agents/build-n-test.md       # Explicit changed paths (absolute OK)\nbb test:routed --dry-run deps.edn               # Print dispatch; executes no gate\nbb test:routed                                 # Git change set, same base as test:changed\nbb deps:npm                                    # Content-checked local npm ci\nbb -cp src/dev -m quick-sharded-test            # Runner/routing/cache self-tests\n```\n\n`test:quick-sharded` is a JVM-only execution alternative for broad G2 and\nthe JVM portion of G3. Without selection flags it selects every JVM test;\n`--changed FILE...` or `--base REF` uses `affected/select` and\n`affected/lane-tests`, exactly as `test:changed:clj` does. Wide input selects\nthe full JVM set here. It never changes `test:changed`'s selection or exit-2\nsemantics. `--workers N` must be positive (default 4 on the laptop).\n\nShard membership comes from every entry in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`, using\n`affected/select-subsystems` and the same longest-prefix ownership rule as\n`test:sub`. As in the shiva script below, currently unowned namespaces run\nin an explicit `unmapped` residual shard, printed by name. Registry omissions\ntherefore cannot silently shrink coverage. Each nonempty shard gets its own\n`clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns> ...` process, with at most N JVMs running.\nPrerequisites are derived from the entire selected JVM reach and finish\nserially before workers start; the Node REPL build prerequisite\n(`build:yin-repl-node`) also ensures `bb deps:npm`.\n\nEach invocation writes a separate `target/profile/quick-sharded-<id>/`:\n`selected.txt`, `ns-<shard>.txt`, `covered.txt`, `uncovered.txt`, per-shard logs,\nand `summary.edn`. Before execution it checks **set equality** between selected\nnamespaces and the union of shard lists, plus absence of duplicate assignments.\nAny uncovered, unexpected, or duplicate namespace fails loudly before tests.\nThe final summary totals tests, assertions, failures and errors and separates\nJVM wall time from total time including selection/builds. A nonzero child exit,\nfailure/error, or missing final test-runner summary makes the task exit nonzero.\nDry plans write namespace evidence but execute no builds or tests. An empty\nselection prints `0 JVM namespaces selected` and exits 1, including dry plans.\nThe final `summary.edn` includes a `:verification-scope` record; the same EDN\nrecord is printed on stdout with `:type :verification-scope`, `:runner`,\n`:scope`, `:exit`, `:dry-run?`, and `:gate-complete?`. JVM feedback always has\n`:scope :jvm-feedback` and `:gate-complete? false`, even on exit 0.\n\n`test:routed <changed-files...>` applies this priority table to the whole change\nset, prints the exact dispatch, executes it and propagates its result:\n\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Change class (first match wins)                                                              | Dispatch                                                              |\n+==============================================================================================+=======================================================================+\n| Existing wide inputs (`deps.edn`, `bb.edn`, `src/dev/`, ANTLR, package locks, runner         | `bb test` — full quick tri-host; success required                     |\n| configs, etc.), toolchain manifests, CI workflows, build scripts                             |                                                                       |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Only `.md`, `.txt`, `.rst` documentation, excluding test resources                           | `git diff --check HEAD -- <paths>` — documentation whitespace/format  |\n|                                                                                              | lint only                                                             |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Foundation code owned by a layer-0 entry in `subsystems.edn`, or `yang.clojure`, `yang.php`, | `bb test:quick-sharded --changed <paths>` — broad selected JVM        |\n| `yang.typescript`, `yang.python` code                                                        | feedback                                                              |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Other code/tests/fixtures with a nonempty affected selection                                 | `bb test:changed --changed <paths>` — existing quick tri-host         |\n|                                                                                              | selection                                                             |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Other non-doc input with no statically selected tests                                        | `bb test` — fail closed rather than report a vacuous green gate       |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nMixed changes take the widest matching route. Missing paths, an empty git change\nset, unknown flags, and the wrong cwd fail. Deleted paths are validated against\nthe base tree. If the leaf command returns exit 2, routing immediately dispatches\n`bb test` and requires its success. `--dry-run`/`--list` only reports the route;\nits zero exit is **not** a verification result. Documentation checks inspect\nstaged and unstaged changes together against HEAD. Paths absent from\n`git ls-files` print `unchecked (untracked)` and appear in the scope record\nunder `:unchecked`; they cannot complete the docs gate. These checks do not lint\nexecutable examples or validate documentation links.\n\nRouting prints its own final EDN verification-scope record. `:gate-complete?`\nmeans the dispatched gate completed successfully, not that every landing gate\nis satisfied. It is false for foundation/JVM feedback, dry runs, unchecked docs,\nand failed dispatches. Successful checked docs, selected tri-host, and full\nquick tri-host routes report `:docs-whitespace`, `:selected-quick-tri-host`, and\n`:full-quick-tri-host` respectively. Exit codes continue to propagate child\nresults, so automation must inspect the scope as well as the exit code.\n\n**Gate requirements are unchanged.** Sharded JVM success alone is not G2/G3\ntri-host success: for foundation/compiler changes also run\n`bb test:changed:cljs --changed <paths>` and\n`bb test:changed:cljd --changed <paths>`, respecting the existing JVM/Dart\nserialization rule. G1, full post-merge `bb test` (G3), applicable slow G4,\ndemo smoke checks and review/landing gates all remain required. Node and Dart\nlanes retain their current commands. The original unsharded tasks remain\navailable. **Individual tests may run on the laptop; suite-scale runs may\nnot (owner ruling 2026-11-04).** A single failing test or one namespace\nreproduces quickly and is fine locally. Everything suite-scale — subsystem\nlane sets (G1), changed-set runs (G2), the full quick suite, the slow tier,\n`bb test:all`, and `nightly.clj` — runs on the remote hosts: the suite has\noutgrown the laptop. The slow tier is 52 long namespaces whose JVM\nprocesses each multi-thread (test workers, JIT, GC threads sized by core\ncount); four of them GC-thrashing at once drove a laptop load average over\n100 on 2026-11-01 while making no progress (the remote hosts run this tier:\njupiter 5,645 s at 12 workers, shiva comparable). Do not run another lane\nset concurrently on the laptop either.\n\n#### Worktree-local prerequisite caches\n\n`bb gen:antlr` now checks a content key over all grammar/manifests (including\nrecorded helper/generated digests and runtime pins), the generator/cache source,\n`deps.edn`, `mise.toml`, actual Java/javac versions and canonical worktree path.\nIt also verifies the names and SHA-256 bytes of all generated targets, compiled\nclasses and cached upstream helpers before a hit. Changed inputs or missing,\nremoved or corrupted outputs trigger a full generation, retaining the generator's\ndigest checks. Filtered/discovery invocations retain their CLI behavior and\ninvalidate the complete-build stamp.\n\n`bb deps:npm` wraps `npm ci` with a key covering `package-lock.json`,\n`package.json`, npm configuration, Node/npm versions, platform, relevant npm/Node\nenvironment and worktree identity; it verifies the installed file tree before\nreuse. A failed command never establishes a cache hit. Existing Node build tasks\nensure this dependency too. Direct `npm ci` is still available.\n\nStamps and fail-fast writer locks live under `target/prereq-cache/`. Remove a\nlock only after confirming its owning process died. Symlinked output parents\nthat escape the worktree are refused. This is reuse **within one worktree**,\nnot a shared artifact store: never share mutable `build/`, `lib/`, `target/` or\n`node_modules/` across live worktrees. ANTLR generation and Dart compilation\nstill require the existing worktree isolation/serialization rules; the cache\ndoes not make overlapping builds and tests safe. Peer executables and shadow\nbuilds themselves are not cached by this mechanism.\n\nLaptop pilot (2026-10-29, 8 cores, 4 workers, `yang.clojure.cljc` changed):\nthe unchanged selector and the sharded plan both covered **61 JVM namespaces**;\nboth completed with **955 tests, 14,848 assertions, zero failures/errors**.\nSharded JVM wall time was **177.165 s** (total including prerequisites **257.207 s**;\nouter command **258.27 s**). The original `test:changed:clj` command completed\nin **376.79 s** overall and reported **298 s** for its JVM lane (that runner\nrounds to whole seconds). The investigation's earlier unsharded command had\ntimed out at 300 s without a summary. These are single-run observations, not\na median/p95 claim or a replacement for tri-host landing verification.\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n   **Failure-reproduction rule (owner ruling 2026-11-01): when a test fails anywhere — a\n   gate, the nightly, or a delegate report — reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the\n   failing test, never the whole suite.** JVM: `clojure -M:test -n <ns>` (a single\n   deftest: add `-v <ns>/<var-name>`). Node: `node target/node-tests.js --test=<ns>`\n   (built by `bb build:yin-repl-node` first only if that test needs the Node REPL).\n   Dart: `bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj --only <ns>`. Fix against the failing test alone,\n   re-run it green, then climb back up (G1 for the home subsystem, G2 for dependents).\n   Never reproduce or chase a failure with the full suite or the slow tier — the full\n   suite is not a debugging tool, and running it locally for one failing test is the\n   exact misuse that drove a laptop load average over 100 (2026-11-01). A failure that\n   only reproduces under the full suite's concurrency is diagnosed with the suite's own\n   logs (its per-shard/per-test output), not by re-running the suite repeatedly.\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master (merged in place by the 2026-11-01 owner ruling)**: The landing gate\n   for a change is the **tests that cover the feature being built** — `bb test:changed`\n   (G2) already includes the reverse-require closure of the changed files, and a wide\n   change still routes to the full quick lane. The complete suite is no longer a\n   per-merge gate: it runs twice a day on the remote hosts (see \"Cadence\" below), so a\n   break is caught within hours without charging every merge ~400 minutes of slow tier.\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge\n   (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n   (owner decision 2026-10-12), and implicitly twice daily by the scheduled run below.\n   G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n  REPL.\n\n```sh\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\n\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\n- **Cadence (owner ruling 2026-11-05):** a merge needs only the tests that cover the\n  feature being built — the affected/dependent set from `bb test:changed` (or\n  `bb test:sub <own> --slow` when the change touches slow-tagged code). The FULL suite,\n  including the slow tier, runs **once a day at 04:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh** as a\n  disjoint lane pair (`src/dev/nightly.clj`): **jupiter takes the jvm lane, shiva\n  takes node and dart** (each lane in its own clone; shiva publishes the run pin,\n  jupiter reads it and falls back to its own current master as an advisory run).\n  The measured long set is ~400 minutes of serial slow-tier work, so it cannot be\n  a per-merge cost; a break is instead caught by the next scheduled run.\n  The schedule is an **hourly cron plus a Vietnam-time guard** in\n  `~/ci/nightly-cron.sh` on each host (the guard fires only at the 04 VN hour and\n  `flock` skips a slot whose previous run is still active), so it is immune to the\n  DST shifts of either host's local timezone. **The cron entries are currently\n  REMOVED (owner instruction 2026-11-06)**: the wrappers remain installed on both\n  hosts and re-arming is one crontab line each\n  (`0 * * * * ~/ci/nightly-cron.sh >> ~/ci/nightly-cron.log 2>&1`). Logs:\n  `~/ci/nightly-cron.log`; results: `~/ci/nightly-results/<stamp>/summary.txt`.\n\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\nservers\" for how to run them):\n\n| Run | Time |\n|---|---|\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n\n```sh\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n```\n\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\nfor s in $SUBS; do\n  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\ndone\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\nfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\nsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\ncomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\necho \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nrun_shard() {\n  s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\nprintf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-6}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nrun_ns() {\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\necho \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nxargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\necho \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\n\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\nand `git`.\n\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (server snapshot checked 2026-10-11; repo selection comes from `mise.toml`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Server snapshot: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1. The repo's `mise.toml` selects Flutter `latest` on beta, not a fixed version. Activate the repo toolchain before running any lane. |\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | Server snapshot (rechecked 2026-10-29): java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.8 (the stale hand-symlinked 3.41.9 was replaced via `mise install flutter@3.47.8` after it failed to compile `dart:ui`-using cljd namespaces; if the Dart lane dies in the cljd compile, check `mise ls flutter` first). Tri-host quick tier verified green on 2026-10-29 (see Measured times). |\n\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\n\n### Kill remote processes by exact PID, never by name pattern\n\nOn these hosts, kill a runaway nightly/lane process by its exact PID\n(`pgrep -af <pattern>` only to LIST, then `kill <pid>`), never with\n`pkill -f <string>` / `killall <name>`. Measured 2026-11-06: `pkill -f\n\"nightly.clj\"` over SSH matched the invoking bash command line itself\n(the pattern appears in `ps` output of the session carrying the kill),\nterminating the SSH session mid-command — exit 255 with the kill\nhalf-done. A name-pattern kill also races concurrent seats: another\norchestrator's delegate process whose arguments embed the same string\ndies with yours. If the PID from `pgrep -af` is ambiguous (several\ncandidates), inspect with `ps -o pid,etime,args -p <pid>` first and kill\nthe exact process; a session that dies under you is a symptom of this\nmistake, not a host outage.\n\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\n\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\n\n```sh\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \"$URL\";\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\n```\n\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\n\n### A clone is only current if it updated from real master\n\nA scratch clone's `origin/master` ref is only as fresh as the place it fetches\nfrom — and a clone made from another scratch clone inherits that clone's *stale*\nrefs. Measured on shiva 2026-11-02: the `dg` clone was created from the `p4`\nscratch clone, whose local `master` ref had not moved since October 26 because\nevery lane on it ran detached off fetched branches; the `dg` lane's\n`checkout origin/master` therefore produced a commit 436 commits behind real\nmaster, and the G3 Dart leg honestly reported failures (the datagram fix) for a\ntree that did not contain them.\n\nBefore any lane runs in a clone — every time, not just the first:\n\n```sh\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\ngit remote get-url origin          # must be the GitHub URL, never another scratch clone\ngit fetch -q origin \"+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*\"\ngit checkout -q --detach origin/master   # or the lane's branch\ngit merge-base --is-ancestor <the-commit-hash-you-pushed> HEAD && echo CURRENT\n```\n\nIf `origin` is not GitHub, repoint it (`git remote set-url origin \"$URL\"`). If\nthe `merge-base` check fails, the clone's bookkeeping lies: refetch, or delete\nand recreate the clone from GitHub directly. Never trust a hash the clone\nreports without comparing it to the hash your push from the laptop printed.\n\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\ninto the clone:\n\n```sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\nmise install                                    # install versions selected by this checkout's mise.toml\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-selected tools on PATH\nmise current flutter                            # verify the selected Flutter version\n```\n\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-selected tools\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\nfind flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n\n\"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\navoidable\"). The laptop additionally never runs the slow tier or the nightly\ndriver (see the sharded-lane section) — a full slow tier there reached a load\naverage over 100 on 2026-11-01; the remote hosts own that tier. On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\n\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n  verified by a measured run.\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\nseparate Dart clone.\n\n**The nightly driver now enforces this by construction.** `src/dev/nightly.clj`\n(2026-11-03, kimi-reviewed) runs a multi-lane invocation as one concurrent\nfuture per lane, each in its OWN clone — jvm keeps the primary clone, node\nand dart get `<primary>-node` / `<primary>-dart`. The pinned SHA is resolved\nonce and checked out in every lane clone; a cache-warm pass\n(`clojure -P -M:test:cljs:clojuredart:cljd`) runs in the primary before\nfan-out so two clones never fetch into the shared `~/.m2`/`~/.gitlibs`\nconcurrently; per-lane summaries are appended serially after the futures\njoin. A single-lane invocation (jupiter's `jvm`) is unchanged: one clone,\none serial lane. Both hosts run the same driver — the lane list on the\ncommand line (shiva: `node,dart` + `--pin-publish`; jupiter: `jvm` +\n`--pin-read --pin-peer shiva...`) is the only difference.\n\n### Scratch-clone first-run gaps (two measured sightings)\n\nA fresh scratch clone needs two things the lane runner does not yet set up\nitself; until the runner heuristic is fixed, do them by hand before the\nfirst lane:\n\n1. **`npm install`** — the Node tests require npm deps (`@noble/hashes` was\n   the measured miss on shiva 2026-11-02: the cljs lane ran but the\n   node-tests bundle died with `MODULE_NOT_FOUND`).\n2. **`bb gen:antlr`** — the `prerequisites` heuristic in\n   `src/dev/affected.clj` decides `:gen:antlr` from the JVM lane's reach\n   only (\"ANTLR classes are JVM only\" — stale: python keeps multi-target\n   parsers), so a cljs/cljd-only selection that reaches\n   `yang.python.antlr.parser` (e.g. `bb test:sub:cljs yang.base` via\n   `yin.repl.frontends`) skips the generation and the shadow build dies on\n   the missing `Python3Lexer.js`. Sighted independently on the p4 lane\n   (PhpLexer ClassNotFoundException) and the yang.base cljs lane\n   (2026-11-02). The lane-runner fix landed 2026-11-03 (eb27fd47).\n\n### Merge verification: run the suite from a fresh clone, not the authoring worktree\n\nA merge whose new code reads a file that was never `git add`ed passes in\nthe authoring worktree (the untracked file is still on disk there) and\nbreaks everywhere else. Measured: the H3-0 landing (1fd7236b) merged\n`kernel-declarations` — which reads\n`yang/latte_oracle/slice9_declarations.edn` via `io/resource` at load —\nwithout the file (untracked in the authoring worktree); master's\nyang.latte-oracle-test died at load until the file was restored\n(2026-11-04). Before landing any merge that adds code reading a new\nresource, or after any merge at all when in doubt:\n\n```sh\ngit worktree add /tmp/merge-verify <merge-commit>\ncd /tmp/merge-verify && <the touched subsystem's focused test>\ngit worktree remove /tmp/merge-verify\n```\n\nA fresh clone/worktree has only committed files — a missing untracked\nresource fails there immediately.\n\n### Ledger commit hygiene: never commit a conflict marker\n\nThe append-only ledgers (`docs/agents/known-gaps.md`,\n`orchestrator-logs/*.md`, `docs/agents/routing-status.md`) resolve rebase\nand merge conflicts by the union rule, which means conflicts are common —\nand a conflict marker that reaches a commit silently corrupts the record\n(measured: the 2026-10-30 rebase landed a log with three committed\n`<<<<<<<`/`>>>>>>>` markers and dropped records the union was supposed to\nkeep; caught by the architect four rounds later). Before committing ANY\nchange to a ledger file:\n\n```sh\ngit diff --cached | grep -E '^\\+(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' && echo CONFLICT-MARKERS-STAGED\ngit grep -E '^(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' -- docs/agents/known-gaps.md orchestrator-logs docs/agents/routing-status.md\n```\n\nIf either hits, the commit stops until the union is redone. When resolving\na ledger conflict, prefer regenerating the union from both sides' section\nlists over hand-merging, and re-read the result against BOTH parents before\nstaging.\n\n### The jolt J2 lane: run it with `--jobs` on the servers\n\nThe jolt lane's core mode (`bb src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj core [--jobs N]\n[--only re] [--timeout-ms M]`) runs each test namespace as its own child jolt\nprocess (own Chez VM, own native parser library load, own log under\n`build/j2-logs/core/`), so the namespaces cannot corrupt one another — the\nonly thing `--jobs N` overlaps is the waiting. The default is `jobs 1`\n(strictly serial), which on jupiter makes the full ~179-namespace lane a\n5-6-hour run.\n\nOn the servers, run it with `--jobs 16`:\n\n- jolt children are essentially single-threaded (the 16 MiB parse thread\n  only burns during parses) and cost 1-2 GB each, so 16 concurrent children\n  sit far inside jupiter's 48 cores / 62 GB and shiva's 24 / 62;\n- the lane's wall time is dominated by a few long namespaces\n  (`yin.repl.python-antlr-test` has an explicit 3-hour step,\n  `yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test` needs one — see\n  `step-timeout-ms` in `src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj`), so beyond ~16 workers\n  the critical path is the longest child, not the count;\n- **parallel is unproven until checked once**: the recorded lane evidence\n  (J2 closure, the safepoint 60/265,225/0 pass) was all produced `jobs 1`.\n  The first parallel run must be compared per-namespace against a serial\n  baseline; any namespace that flips red gets one serial re-run to\n  attribute before its evidence is trusted. Until that first comparison\n  passes, keep `jobs 1` for anything whose verdict is timing-sensitive —\n  timeout and wall-time measurements, native-memory/leak gates, and the\n  merge-decision qualification runs.\n\nSuites that exceed the lane's default step budget are pinned in the lane's\n`step-timeout-ms` map with their measured wall time; a namespace timing out\nunder the default budget is a reason to measure it serially and add an\nentry, not to re-run under the same bound.\n\n### The full run\n\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (both hosts run all three lanes; a second clone for Dart is recommended)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\nDART=\"${2:-}\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nLOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n         echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\nif [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n  ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\nfi\nwait\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n```sh\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n```\n\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n(measured 2026-10-11).\n\n### Known differences from the laptop\n\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` on Linux —\n  MEASURED AND FIXED (2026-11-02).** The old entry inferred that \"a second UDP bind on\n  a taken port behaves differently on Linux\"; confirmed, with the cause. Dart's\n  `RawDatagramSocket.bind` defaults to `reuseAddress: true`, and **Linux honours\n  `SO_REUSEADDR` for UDP**, so a second bind to a port a reuse-enabled socket already\n  holds *succeeds* (`strace`: `SO_REUSEADDR=1` on both sockets, no `SO_REUSEPORT`, both\n  kernel `bind()` calls succeed); macOS refuses with errno 48. The seam was correct\n  throughout — given a genuinely exclusive holder (`reuseAddress: false`) it deposits\n  `bind-failed` in ~10 ms with errno 98. The test asserted one host's binding policy\n  rather than the contract, so it now binds its own exclusive blocker\n  (`RawDatagramSocket.bind \"127.0.0.1\" 0 .reuseAddress false .reusePort false`) and\n  points the seam at that port: the failure path is exercised on both hosts. Verified\n  on shiva: the namespace passes 5/5.\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n\n### The nightly full suite and the branch-testing rule (2026-10-30 owner ruling)\n\nOwner, verbatim: \"since the complete test can take a good 7-8hrs to run\ncompletely, it should be schedule to run once a day at 3am. while\nbranch/worktree test should only run test that are targeting code\nchanges\" — and, clarifying the nightly's target: \"it should not be for\nonly a worktree/branch. the nightly complete test should be for master.\"\n\nTwo tiers, by target and purpose:\n\n- **The nightly full suite runs MASTER ONLY** (`~/ci/nightly-cron.sh` on\n  shiva and jupiter, hourly cron plus a 04:00 Vietnam guard and flock,\n  documented under \"Cadence\" above; the driver fetches and checks out\n  origin/master before anything runs). Every lane, across the host pair:\n  the JVM full suite on jupiter, the full Node and Dart suites on shiva.\n  Its results are a\n  post-hoc integrity net for master, NOT a landing gate. Failures it\n  finds route to fix rounds the next morning.\n- **Branch/worktree testing runs only the namespaces that target the\n  changed code** (the diff's own tests plus the surfaces the diff\n  touches), one JVM per namespace, plus one cheap portable lane (Node)\n  when cljc goldens or fixtures moved. Never the full tier on a branch:\n  a branch gate needs the changed code proven, not the whole tree\n  re-proven. A branch is never promoted by its own testing — the merge\n  to master is what puts it under the nightly.\n\nAn orchestrator landing a slice cites the subset runs plus the gate\nsign-off; the nightly catches what the subset could not see, and a red\nnightly owns the follow-up.\n\n**The merge precondition (2026-10-30 owner ruling, restored 2026-10-31 —\na dedup pass dropped this section along with a stale duplicate of the\ntwo-tier policy; both stand):** before any merge into master, the\n`dao.stream` tests must run and pass — dao.stream is what everything is\nbuilt on. Concretely: `bb test:sub dao.stream` (the subsystem's tests,\n`--slow` when the slice touches slow-guarded surfaces) is part of every\nbranch gate regardless of which subsystems the diff's own changes\nselect, because a change can break the foundation without touching it.\nA red dao.stream blocks the merge until fixed or diagnosed as\npre-existing by the owning seat.\n\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\nextra cores, and measure before relying on a speedup. (Historical record — the\n\"free laptop\" comparison predates the 2026-11-04 ruling that suite-scale runs\nbelong on the remote hosts.)\n\n### Jupiter verification run (2026-10-29, commit 7c0a078f, scratch clone, sequential lanes)\n\nFirst full tri-host verification of jupiter. Prerequisites (npm ci, Node REPL,\nANTLR via `gen:antlr`, Dart peer) all succeeded; the stale 3.41.9 Flutter was\nreplaced with 3.47.8 before the Dart lane. Results: **JVM quick tier\n(`test:quick-sharded`, 12 workers) rc=0 in 702 s** (cold, every shard green);\n**Node quick tier rc=0 in 4583 s** (all tests pass, but roughly 15x the\nlaptop — route Node lanes to shiva; the laptop comparison is historical and\nsuite-scale Node runs no longer belong there per the 2026-11-04 ruling);\n**Dart quick tier\nrc=1 in 1495 s with the ONLY failure the documented known Linux-only red**\n(`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events`, same\ntaken-port/bind-failed signature recorded above). Routing summary: jupiter\nis the preferred JVM/sharding box (48 cores); Node stays off it when wall\ntime matters; Dart works on either host. Timings are single cold runs, not\nmedians, and two foreign QEMU guests idle on the host.\n\nOne exception to the concurrency above: never run two lanes that both trigger\n`gen:antlr` (the quick `test:cljs`/`test:cljd` tasks) in the SAME worktree at the\nsame time — they race on `build/antlr` and `lib/antlr` and fail with\n\"Patch does not apply\" or \"Generated sources differ from the recorded digests\".\nRun them sequentially, or in separate clones (as the slow-tier harness does).\nThe fix after a collision is `rm -rf build/antlr lib/antlr` and a rerun; the\ngenerator is not idempotent over an already-patched tree. Corroborated twice in\none week (the M3 round and the C3 completion round, 2026-10-18).\n\n### Remote agent execution (delegate CLIs on shiva and jupiter)\n\nOwner relay, 2026-10-24: the delegate agent CLIs — `claude`, `codex`, `glm` —\nare installed and usable **on the remote hosts themselves**, not only from the\norchestrator's laptop. This generalizes this section from \"run tests remotely\nover SSH\" to \"delegate whole work agents remotely\": a heavy implementation or\nverification leg (test lanes, native builds, fault-injection runs, multi-hour\nsuites) can be executed by a delegate agent running on the server, keeping the\norchestrator's laptop unloaded.\n\nThe pattern (composed from the rules above plus the delegate-invocation\nreference):\n\n1. **Land the branch on origin first** (push from the laptop; never rsync) and\n   prepare the scratch clone per \"Get the code there\".\n2. **Start a named tmux session** for the task; run the agent inside it with\n   the toolchain activated *inside* the tmux command (`eval \"$(mise env -s\n   bash)\"` after the `cd` — the non-interactive SSH shell does not have it).\n   A fresh clone hits mise's interactive trust prompt, which blocks the whole\n   session until answered via `tmux send-keys`; pre-empt it with `mise trust`\n   inside the same command.\n3. **The remote agent works in the clone**: implement, build, run lanes,\n   write its findings artifact — the same brief form as any delegate dispatch\n   (role template, bounded file ownership, deliverable path), just executing\n   on the host. Long jobs survive SSH drops because of tmux.\n4. **The orchestrator polls detached files, never the pane** (pane captures\n   carry control characters that make output look empty): rc files, summary\n   logs and `ls *.flag` sentinels written by the run.\n5. **Division of labor**: bookkeeping and polling are orchestrator SSH\n   one-liners; execution belongs to the remote agent. Do not run heavy lanes\n   from the orchestrator's laptop while a remote agent owns the same gate.\n6. **Same trust rules as any delegate**: remote execution does not waive\n   Workflow steps 6–7 of the orchestrator role — artifacts are verified, and\n   review before commit applies to remote-produced work identically.\n   Session-ID capture and resume rules from the delegate-invocation reference\n   apply per host (each host's `~/.claude`, `~/.codex` store is its own).\n\nKnown state: `claude` and `codex` verified working on shiva (2026-10-24, the\njolt canonical-fixes verification ran there); `glm` reported available by the\nowner on both hosts, not yet exercised. Both hosts are toolchain-verified for\nthe full tri-host suite (jupiter verified 2026-10-29 — see Measured times;\nits 3.41.9 Flutter was replaced with 3.47.8, and model availability per\nhost is governed by `routing-status.md`, not this file).\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-CM5k9qf1' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-11-06 15:20:51.980 xcodebuild[19719:112232331]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-11-06 15:20:52.280 xcodebuild[19719:112232330] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-dpLjj5li' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-11-06 15:20:53.109 xcodebuild[19724:112232372]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-11-06 15:20:53.352 xcodebuild[19724:112232369] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\n?? .flutter-plugins-dependencies\n?? .jolt/cpcache/k270626430.edn\n?? BRIEF-j6g-mob.md\n?? collab/1793546000000-engineer-node-shard-uncovered-skip.kimi-k3.1-pro.findings.md\n?? collab/1793546000000-engineer-node-shard-uncovered-skip.kimi-k3.1-pro.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793546000000-engineer-node-shard-uncovered-skip.prompt.md\n?? collab/1794530000000-engineer-hooks-fixture-fix.gpt-6-luna.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794400000000-architect-eval-bridge-design.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794410000000-engineer-p8-manifest-repair.muse-spark-1.3.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794430000000-engineer-p8-manifest-repair.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794450000000-engineer-p8-manifest-repair-r2.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794470000000-engineer-p8-manifest-repair-r3.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794520000000-engineer-p5-oracle-record.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794530000000-engineer-p8-pin-fix-laptop.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794550000000-engineer-p8-pin-fix-r2.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794560000000-engineer-p5-runner.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794580000000-reviewer-retro-step7.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794590000000-engineer-cellref-sweep.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794600000000-engineer-roundA-p1-fixes.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794610000000-engineer-cellref-sweep-r2.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794630000000-engineer-roundA-r2.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794680000000-architect-consolidated-round.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794690000000-architect-safepoint-slice3-design.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794700000000-engineer-p1-followup.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794700000001-engineer-roundB1-p9-contracts.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794710000000-engineer-p5-fold-fixes.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794720000000-architect-confirm-round.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794750000000-engineer-roundB1-r2.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794820000000-architect-confirm3.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794860000000-architect-confirm4.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794880000000-engineer-notice-fix.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794900000000-architect-p8-final-confirm.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794950000000-engineer-p8-final-items.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794990000000-architect-p8-final-confirm2.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1795020000000-engineer-p8-fixes2.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1795060000000-architect-p8-final-confirm3.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/orch-lane/1795090000000-engineer-p8-reconcile.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1793952867000-orchestrator-jolt-to-yangclojure-j2-probe-results.md\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1793953025937-reviewer-h3-6-audit.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1794700000000-reviewer-aaq-ruling.codex.stdout.log\n?? orchestrator-logs/1793947104-agy-gemini-3.8-flash-yang-clojure-seat-takeover.md\n?? orchestrator-logs/1793952675-agy-gemini-3.8-flash-jolt-seat-takeover.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
