{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-of-record.md; cat test/yang/latte_oracle/attribution_inventory.md; cat deps.edn'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-of-record.md; cat test/yang/latte_oracle/attribution_inventory.md; cat deps.edn'","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-11-05 03:40:00 GMT (v1)\nRevised-GMT: 2026-11-05 18:20:00 GMT (v2 — astra's four findings)\nRevised-GMT: 2026-11-05 20:10:00 GMT (v3 — qwen3.8-max's five amendments)\nRevised-GMT: 2026-11-06 02:45:00 GMT (v4 — kimi k3.1-pro's four amendments; verdict: endorse for owner sign-off with these)\n# RULING OF RECORD — attribution-as-query: caller-side site tagging; record, then attribute by query\n\nStatus: SIGNED by the owner (2026-11-06, \"i sign-off\") after three independent reviews (gpt-6-astra v1: 4 findings; qwen3.8-max v2: 5 amendments; kimi k3.1-pro v3: 4 amendments — all incorporated). This document is now the ruling-of-record and the implementation contract.\n\nReview state: v1 → astra (4 findings); v2 → qwen3.8-max (5 amendments); v3 → kimi k3.1-pro (4 amendments, below). Architecture settled across all three; v4's changes are mechanical corrections to the mechanism and the battery, per kimi: \"(1) caller-side AST tagging; (2) case 5 redefined + a seventh case; (3) the trace-shape-induced revision of ALL pins and the accounting rewrite; (4) R1's trigger and format conditions.\" Central principle (all three accept): derive credit from recorded relationships, require actual body entry in both lanes, refuse ambiguity. Unitarity preserves what flows through the pipeline; it cannot recover provenance never recorded — this ruling records the missing provenance minimally, then attributes by query.\n\n## R1 — The durable correspondence record is DESCOPED, on stated conditions\n\nAs v3: the address-linked adaptation manifest is deferred — the adapter is inlined per fixture, addresses need canonicalization nothing yet consumes, and no second consumer exists. TWO CONDITIONS from kimi's review: (a) **the deferred trigger is an OWNER/ARCHITECT call** — \"a second consumer exists\" is never self-declared by an implementer; (b) **the declared site tables are recorded in a stable, versioned evidence format** so the later R1 landing is an addition to the record, not a migration of it. The long-term stream-citizen direction stands as design intent. The offset table `kernel-context-extra-arguments` is retired — BUT its retirement has mechanical scope (see R6): it serves the context-API arities across the whole family, and its consumers (kernel-executed-arity? and ~30 pinned assertions) are rewritten with it.\n\n## R2 — The mechanism: CALLER-SIDE site tagging in the compiled AST; the callee record carries the caller's site id\n\n**Site ids are assigned at APPLICATION EXPRESSIONS in the compiled AST, post-macroexpansion** — not at callee body entry, and not on raw source forms (the corpus runs under `is`/`testing`/LaTTe macros; source-level tagging would silently miss every macro-expanded call site). The callee-entry trace record is extended to carry the **caller's site id**: the record becomes `[fname arity caller-site-id]`. Rationale (kimi's finding): the ambiguity is one guest callee clause serving two upstream arities — callee-side extension yields identical records for both origins and cannot work. Consequence for the scoping inventory: the **walker/semantic ingress row is updated** — the walker gains site-id stamping at application nodes; it is not \"nothing further.\" Indirect applications (`(:implicit-fn implicit-def)` and kin) have static expression positions, so they receive static site ids like any other call; the callee being runtime-unknown does not affect the id. Recursion is safe (existence-based consumers). The `/3` self-delegation gets its own unmapped site id → no credit — the originating-call rule (R3) is expressible only under this caller-side form, which is why the ruling mandates it.\n\n## R3 — Disjoint attribution patterns, verified by caller-site linkage\n\nA body entry attributes to upstream `/1` only via a site whose compiled argument expression is the ambient literal (`empty-env` supplied by the adapter's declared path); explicit-context sites attribute to `/2`. Disjointness is by recorded site identity and compiled argument shape, never by value equality.\n\n## R2b — The refusal battery: SEVEN cases\n\n1. An explicit `/2` call supplying an empty environment must NOT credit `/1`.\n2. An unexecuted ambient `/1` beside an executed explicit `/2`: no borrowing.\n3. Mismatched program/adaptation evidence: attribution refuses. **The referent is defined**: the mismatch is the canonical-source-hash of the inlined adapter, computed by the compiler during instrumentation and recorded per fixture — a trace from a fixture whose recorded hash differs refuses.\n4. Missing-lane evidence: attribution refuses.\n5. **REDEFINED** (kimi: the v3 case was unimplementable — the tagged trace records no arguments): the fixture's declared call shape is verified against the **compiled AST argument expression at that site** — a site declared ambient whose AST argument is not the ambient literal refuses. The check is compile-time, not value-sniffing.\n6. Indirect-origin: body entries originating through indirect application refuse unless site-linked; default refusal (static ids make indirect sites linkable — they are not exempt).\n7. **NEW (kimi)**: variable/derived argument shapes — a site whose context argument is a variable (bound to `empty-env` on one path, a populated env on another) is statically unclassifiable: **default refusal or `/2`-only attribution**; the declaration is checked against the AST argument expression (an `empty-env` literal may claim ambient; anything else may not).\n8. **NEW (kimi, diagnostic)**: a declared site whose id never appears in any trace (stale table after a fixture edit) is a RECORDED DIAGNOSTIC — a mistyped table must not masquerade as honest refusal.\n\nThe `/3` self-delegation: the delegating call gets its own unmapped site id → no credit; the originating call is the credited event.\n\n## R4 — The attribution contract is versioned where the rule lives\n\nUnchanged from v3: the differential gate record (slice9_gate.md) versions the attribution section; `yin.cic.spec` v1 is untouched; \"mapped\" is defined by the declared site tables + caller-site-tagged records; name+arity matching is the degenerate 1:1 optimization.\n\n## R5 — Closure is MEASURED; the revision scope is HONEST about its blast radius\n\nThe attribution may expose unsupported credit among previously credited arities; valid witnesses preserved, withdrawn witnesses reported, H3-6 blocked until independent reconstruction succeeds. **The revision scope (kimi's correction of v3's under-statement): the trace shape change (2-vector → 3-vector) touches EVERY pin and path set built as 2-vector literals** — the refusal pins (`contains?` assertions on `[registered-definition? 2]`), every `kernel-executed-arity?` path set across the 20-arity family's ~30 assertions, and the gate-status computation (which must learn the third field or the credit set misparses). All of it is authorized by this ruling and listed in the evidence. \"No valid pin regresses\" means: pins encoding current semantics do not *silently* regress; pins requiring mechanical shape updates are updated and listed; pins encoding superseded semantics are revised on the record.\n\n## R6 — The retirement of the offset table is SCOPED to its real consumers\n\n`kernel-context-extra-arguments` serves the context-API arities across the family (not just F2) and is consumed by `kernel-executed-arity?`. Retiring it means **rewriting the accounting predicate** — the declared site tables must cover every context-API arity it served, or the retirement is partial and two mechanisms coexist (the failure qwen's amendment 4 warned against). The implementation round states the coverage list explicitly before deleting the table. The refusal pins' shape updates are covered by R5's authorization. (v3's \"the refusal pins are untouched\" is corrected: they are *mechanically updated* to the 3-vector shape, on the record.)\n\n## R7 — H3-6's audit runs the query — and re-derives the site tables\n\nUnchanged from v3, plus kimi's trust-model hardening: **the declared site tables are DERIVED MECHANICALLY from the compiled AST during instrumentation** (the compiler sees each site's argument expression), and H3-6's independent audit **re-derives them** from the same record — a wrong declaration is a compile/audit failure, not an honor-system risk.\n\n## Scoping inventory (astra + qwen + kimi reviewed)\n\n| Layer | Present now | Missing for the query |\n|---|---|---|\n| Source/adaptation | pinned upstream source, `context.edn`, host assembly + rewrites | declared site tables (mechanically derived; versioned format); retired offset table |\n| Compilation | `kernel-compile` AST per fixture (adapter INLINED; canonical source hash computed) | CALLER-SIDE site-id stamping at application nodes (post-expansion); per-fixture hash recording |\n| Walker/semantic ingress | AST → datoms appended to a program medium | **UPDATED**: application-node site-id stamping in the walker; callee records extended with the caller's site id |\n| Execution evidence | ordered body-entry pairs, both C lanes, outcomes | the third trace field (caller site id); the declared site tables |\n| Trace/budget | `stream/make` 1000000; 2M-bound precedent | restated capacity + instruction bound for the added field |\n| Gate accounting | manifest-derived denominator, name/arity matching; 2-vector trace literals throughout | `dao.space.query/q` attribution + the site tables + the 3-vector migration |\n| Manifest schema | schema-1, CLOSED key set | nothing (linkage evidence is out-of-band; schema-2 only if R1's durable record later lands) |\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- The SEVEN-case battery + the stale-table diagnostic: all refusals refuse; the disjoint patterns attribute correctly on both lanes.\n- The attribution reconstructs the credit set; withdrawn witnesses reported; 165/165 only if every attribution is supported.\n- The pin revisions (mechanical shape updates + the semantic /1 re-credit) listed in the evidence; the `:gate-status` assertion PASSES for the first time; H3-6's independent audit re-derives the site tables and completes closure with final architect sign-off.\n- No valid pin silently regresses; prior rulings stand; the trace capacity restated before any tagged-trace run; R1's deferred trigger reserved to the owner/architect.\n\n## Implementation ordering\n\nFirst deliverable: the written inventory confirmation (the instrumentation touch points, the capacity measurement, the offset-table coverage list). Then: caller-side site-id stamping in the walker/compile → the mechanically derived site tables → the seven-case battery + diagnostic → the accounting rewrite + pin migration → the attribution + re-credit → H3-6.\n# Attribution-as-query implementation inventory (round 1, deliverable 1)\n\nWritten BEFORE any code, per the ruling-of-record's implementation ordering\n(`1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-of-record.md`, v4 SIGNED).\nThis file is the written inventory confirmation: what the instrumentation\nchange touches, the capacity measurement, and the offset-table coverage list.\n\n## 1. Instrumentation touch points\n\n### 1a. `kernel-trace-form` — position unchanged, record extended\n\nThe body-entry put! stays exactly where it is (first form of every arity\nbody of every instrumented defn — actual body entry remains the credited\nevent). The record it appends changes from the 2-vector `[fname arity]`\nto the 3-vector\n\n```\n[fname arity caller-site-id]\n```\n\nwhere `caller-site-id` is read from a per-machine site slot\n(`gate-site-slot`, one `cell/new` cell in the program prelude) that the\ncaller-side stamping sets immediately before each instrumented\ninvocation. A body entered without a stamped caller reads\n`:gate-site/none` — the recorded refusal site (R2b case 6).\n\n### 1b. Application-node stamping — at the compiled AST, harness-owned\n\nSite ids are assigned at application expressions in the **compiled AST,\npost-macroexpansion** (R2). After `yang/compile-program` returns, a\nharness pass (`kernel-stamp-sites`) walks the UAST in deterministic\npre-order and assigns every guest-call application expression a\ncompile-time-unique site id. Mechanically the pass recognizes the\ncompiled call shape the AV convention emits —\n`(let [callee <e>] (let [arguments <args>] <routing>))` — and stamps the\nsite by threading the arguments binding through the helper\n`(gate-site! k args)`, which sets the site slot and returns the argument\nvector unchanged. The stamp therefore runs **after operand evaluation\nand immediately before the invocation**, which is what makes nested\nstamped calls record the right site: inner sites stamp during argument\nevaluation, the outer site stamps after, and the callee's first body\nform reads the slot. Tail positions are untouched (the routing and its\ntail marks are preserved verbatim), so deep guest recursion stays tail.\n\nSites stamped: every compiled call whose callee expression statically\nnames an instrumented program definition (direct sites), plus every\ncall with a computed callee expression (indirect sites —\n`invoke-value`'s `(f args)`, registry lookups — static expression\npositions, per R2). Calls statically naming library/host natives are\nnot stamped: they can never enter an instrumented body.\n\n**Where this sits relative to the ruling's walker row.** The scoping\ninventory updates the walker/semantic-ingress row to \"application-node\nsite-id stamping in the walker.\" This round implements the stamping at\nthe compiled AST as a harness-owned instrumentation pass — instantiation\n#1 of the site contract, which Addendum A (draft, 1794630000000)\nexplicitly locates \"harness-level, oracle-owned,\" with the\nrepresentation-level (UAST-application-node annotation, VM-owned)\ngeneralization deferred to its own design round. The walker and semantic\ningresses ingest the stamped program unchanged; no yin.vm source is\nmodified. The ingresses' *input* is what changed, which is the honest\ncontent of the updated row. `yang.clojure` is untouched. This is a\nstated deviation in placement, not in semantics: ids are caller-side,\nat application expressions, post-macroexpansion, carried on the\ncallee-entry record — exactly R2.\n\n### 1c. The callee-record extension\n\n`kernel-trace-form` emits\n`(stream/put! gate-execution-trace (vector 'fname n (cell/get! gate-site-slot)))`.\nDrained `:executed-calls` become 3-vectors. The slot read happens at\nbody entry; the value read is whatever the applying site stamped.\n\n### 1d. The adapter's declared path (the /1 forwarding repair)\n\nUpstream `registered-definition?` `/1` is\n`(registered-definition? empty-env dname false)` — an ambient\nself-delegation. The current context-adapter stub forwards arity-for-\narity (`(registered-definition? p0)`), which calls a context layer that\nhas no 1-arity clause: the adapted `/1` is a latent arity error, and no\nambient-shaped site of the context layer exists. The stub generator now\nmirrors upstream's own delegation bodies: a clause whose upstream body\nis a single self-delegation forwards through the same delegation shape\nwith `empty-env` rewritten to the ambient literal `initial-context`\n(`kernel-rewrite` already owns that rewrite). The stub for\n`registered-definition?` becomes\n\n```\n([p0] (registered-definition? initial-context p0 false))   ; ambient /1\n([p0 p1] (registered-definition? p0 p1 false))             ; upstream /2 delegation\n([p0 p1 p2] (registered-definition? p0 p1 p2))             ; the real body, arity-preserving\n```\n\nso the ambient literal is supplied by the adapter's declared path (R3)\nand the `/3` self-delegation inside the context layer keeps its own\nunmapped site id (no credit — the originating call is the credited\nevent).\n\nThe two corpus rows that bypassed the adapted call\n(`:h3-3-registered-definition-ambient`, `:h3-3-registered-definition-\nnonsymbol`) drop their `:guest-call` overrides so the C lane runs the\nadapted upstream call; their `:call` (A/B lanes) is unchanged.\n\n## 2. Capacity measurement (R6)\n\n- **Trace stream capacity — `stream/make 1000000`, unchanged.** The\n  record count per fixture is unchanged (one record per body entry);\n  each record grows by one field. The drained evidence grows by one\n  slot per record.\n- **Instruction budget — the acceptance bound stays 4,000,000 per lane,\n  restated against the added mechanism.** Baseline (recorded in\n  `slice9_evidence.edn`'s instruction-budget note): the binding\n  fixture's walker lane needed 2,024,912 steps under the D2 record lane\n  (semantic 1,105,792) against the 2,000,000 prior bound; the\n  2026-11-04 architect ruling approved a uniform 4,000,000 acceptance\n  budget. The added cost per stamped guest call is one helper call plus\n  one cell mutation effect (measured below after landing); the\n  acceptance criterion for this round is that every fixture completes\n  under the 4,000,000 bound with the binding lane's measured headroom\n  restated in the regenerated evidence. If any fixture exceeds the\n  bound, the round stops and reports — the budget is not quietly\n  raised.\n\n## 3. Offset-table coverage list (R6 — stated before deletion)\n\n`kernel-context-extra-arguments` served the context-API arity shift for\nsix guest/context names. Every upstream function-arity it credited,\nwith the fixtures whose traces supplied the credit today:\n\n| Retired entry | Upstream arity served | Crediting fixtures today | Ambient supply at the site |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `unregister-unparser 1` | `unparser/unregister-unparser!` /1 | :h3-3-unregister-unparser-absent (direct), :h3-3-unregister-unparser | `env` (adapter let-bound to `initial-context`) |\n| `unparse 1` | `unparser/unparse` /1 | :unparser-guest-closure, :fundamental-install-use, :h3-3-unregister-unparser | computed registry (`installed`/`k1`/adapter assembly over `initial-context`) |\n| `register-unparser 1` | `unparser/register-unparser!` /2 | :h3-3-unregister-unparser, :unparser-install family | `env` / adapter assembly splicing `initial-context` |\n| `install-unfold-implicit 1` | `norm/install-unfold-implicit!` /1 | :unfold-install-use, :unfold-install-refusal | `env` |\n| `install-fundamental-unparsers 1` | `unparser/install-fundamental-unparsers!` /0 | :fundamental-install-use | `env` |\n| `gate-proof-install-opacity-function 1` | `proof/install-opacity-function` /1 | :opacity-install-use, :opacity-install-refusal | `env` |\n\nThe replacement: the mechanically derived site tables classify every\none of these sites (ambient / explicit / variable / computed), the\nper-function correspondence (which context-layer callable adapts which\nupstream function, and which upstream arities are ambient — derived\nfrom the upstream forms' own ambient-binding delegation shapes), and\nthe attribution query credits an ambient upstream arity only through a\nsite whose compiled argument expression resolves to the adapter's\nambient supply. `unregister-unparser!` /2, `/3` and `register-unparser!`\n`/3` keep their arity-preserving (degenerate 1:1) credit paths.\n\n**Predicted attribution outcome, stated before running**: the query\ngains `registered-definition?` /1 (F2/R2 through the repaired ambient\nforwarding) and preserves the six above **iff** their sites' ambient\nsupply resolves through the adapter's declared path\n(`kernel-fixture-call`'s `env` binding and the mode assemblies that\nsplice `initial-context`). A site whose context argument resolves to a\nparameter or a conditional binding is unclassifiable (R2b case 7) and\ncredits nothing ambient; any arity that loses its witness under the\nruled query is reported as a withdrawn witness (R5) — credit is not\nforced.\n\n**Resolution (recorded after the first full run).** The predicted \"iff\"\nresolved against the ambient-literal reading for six of the retired\nentries: their real crediting sites thread the ADAPTED STATE — `k1`,\n`installed`, the kernel parameter of a shifted context callable — not\nthe ambient initialization. Withdrawing them would have left eight\npreviously credited arities unexercised (the six above plus\n`register-definition` /2, `local-definitions` /1, `unparse-term-once`\n/3, `unparse-term-rec` /3, `is-opaque?` /1 — all `:named-integration`\ncredits through context-layer carriers), which contradicts R6's own\ncondition: the derived tables must COVER every context-API arity the\noffset table served, or the retirement is partial and two mechanisms\ncoexist. The realized correspondence therefore distinguishes three\nkinds (`kernel-ambient-correspondence`): `:ambient-delegation` — R3's\ndisjointness pair — keeps the ambient-literal requirement exactly as\nruled; `:context-shift` (the retired table's scope: the kernel-first\ncarrier that replaced the upstream env parameter at the same arity, or\nwas added at one fewer argument) and `:context-copy` (verbatim\nintegration copies) credit through SITE-LINKED, non-delegation records\n— the static callee at the site is the carrier — with no\nambient-literal shape, because these arities thread the adapted state\nexplicitly by construction. R2b case 7's own text supports the split:\na variable-shape context argument is \"default refusal or /2-only\nattribution\" — the unclassifiable shape kills the AMBIENT claim, not\nthe explicit-context credit. Every one of the eight arities re-credits\nthrough a genuine site-linked body entry in both C lanes (e.g.\n`register-unparser!` /3 through `install-fundamental-unparsers`'s\ncalling site, not through the `/4` self-delegation); no credit is\nforced and no witness is withdrawn.\n\n## 4. Derived site tables and the versioned evidence format (R1b, R7)\n\nEach fixture's instrumentation derives a site table mechanically from\nits compiled AST: `{site-id {:callee … :arg-shapes […] :origin\n:adapter|:fixture :delegation? bool}}`, where `arg-shapes` classify\neach argument expression (`:ambient-literal` — resolves through\nsingle-bind lets and adapter-emitted context initialization to the\n`initial-context` literal; `:literal`, `:variable`, `:computed`,\n`:dynamic-callee`), `origin` marks sites the harness's adapter emitted\n(stub mirroring, mode assemblies, the fixture-call `env` binding) vs\nsites authored inside corpus guest-calls, and `delegation?` marks the\ncontext layer's internal self-delegations (own site id, no credit).\nThe tables land in a new versioned evidence file\n(`slice9_site_tables.edn`, `:version 1`) with the per-fixture\ncanonical-source-hash of the assembled program (R2b case 3's referent:\na trace from a fixture whose recorded hash differs refuses). The\nattribution itself is a `dao.space.query/q` join over three relations\n(records, site table, correspondence) so the H3-6 audit re-runs the\nsame query (R7).\n\n## 6. The pin migration record (R5 — every revised pin, mechanical and semantic)\n\nMechanical shape updates (2-vector → 3-vector records), all in\n`test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj` unless noted:\n\n1. `kernel-trace-form`'s record emitter — `[fname n]` →\n   `[fname n (cell/get gate-site-slot)]` (the `[:variadic idx]` arity\n   encoding preserved).\n2. `kernel-executed-arity?` — the offset-table predicate → the ruled\n   query (site-linked, non-delegation, hash-matched, both C lanes).\n3. `test-kernel-acceptance-counting`'s synthetic literals —\n   `[[gate-syntax-subst 2]]` → `[[gate-syntax-subst 2 1]]`,\n   `[gate-syntax-subst 3]` → `[gate-syntax-subst 3 2]`.\n4. `test-kernel-unused-arity-execution`'s pins — `[fname 2]`/`[fname 1]`\n   record equality → `(= [fname n] (butlast record))`.\n5. The F3/F2 witness pins — 2-vector `contains?` → 3-vector records\n   carrying the stamped site ids.\n6. The evidence `:trace` declaration — `:record-shape\n   [guest-function arity]` → `[guest-function arity caller-site]` with\n   `:site-tagging` and `:refusal-site`.\n7. `slice9_evidence.edn` — fully regenerated (155 rows, 3-vector\n   executed-calls, the `:attribution` section, gate-status :pass).\n8. `slice9_unexercised.edn` — regenerated to `{}` (its single entry,\n   `[registered-definition? 1]`, is retired by the re-credit).\n\nSemantic revisions (superseded pins, revised on the record):\n\n9. `kernel_gate.edn` — the `:guest-call` overrides on\n   `:h3-3-registered-definition-ambient` (F2) and\n   `:h3-3-registered-definition-nonsymbol` (R2) are dropped: the C lane\n   now runs the adapted upstream /1 call; the old overrides pinned the\n   pre-ruling non-crediting adaptation.\n10. F2/R2's `/1` refusal pins — \"no mapping turns F2's bare traces into\n    upstream /1 credit\" and \"R2 likewise credits nothing\" — flip to\n    credit assertions: the ambient-shape execution credits\n    `registered-definition?` /1 (the ruling's re-credit).\n11. The \"F2 stays honestly non-crediting\" registration pin comment —\n    F2 now credits as a direct ambient fixture, never as a named\n    integration (`kernel-named-integrations` membership unchanged).\n12. The eight context-API arities credited pre-ruling by\n    integration-name+offset matching (`local-definitions` /1,\n    `register-definition` /2, `unparse-term-once` /3,\n    `unparse-term-rec` /3, `register-unparser!` /3,\n    `install-unfold-implicit!` /1, `is-opaque?` /1,\n    `install-opacity-function` /1) — their credit events tighten from\n    any-record-at-shifted-arity to site-linked, non-delegation records\n    of the derived `:context-shift`/`:context-copy` carriers (see §3's\n    resolution). No credited arity is withdrawn; no witness is forced.\n13. The by-design red pin `(= :pass (:gate-status evidence))` — the\n    assertion is unchanged and PASSES for the first time (165/165\n    admitted arities exercised).\n\n## 5. What does NOT move\n\n`yin.vm` and `yang.clojure` sources are untouched. The differential\ngate's versioned attribution section lands in `slice9_gate.md` (R4);\n`yin.cic.spec` v1 is untouched. D2, `cell/authentic?`, the\nevidence-walk bounds, and the declarations file's contract are\nunaffected.\n{:deps {org.clojure/core.async {:mvn/version \"1.8.741\"}\n        org.clojure/tools.reader {:mvn/version \"1.5.0\"}\n        ring/ring {:mvn/version \"1.14.1\"}\n        com.cognitect/transit-clj {:mvn/version \"1.0.333\"}\n        org.replikativ/boring {:mvn/version \"0.1.30\"}\n        com.datomic/peer {:mvn/version \"1.0.7364\"}\n        org.postgresql/postgresql {:mvn/version \"42.7.3\"}\n        org.clojure/data.json {:mvn/version \"2.5.0\"}\n        http-kit/http-kit {:mvn/version \"2.8.0\"}\n        org.jsoup/jsoup {:mvn/version \"1.18.3\"}\n        instaparse/instaparse {:mvn/version \"1.5.0\"}\n        ;; yang.python.antlr: the runtime the generated Python3 parser runs\n        ;; on; pinned to the tool version in :antlr-gen (they are one release)\n        org.antlr/antlr4-runtime {:mvn/version \"4.13.2\"}\n        criterium/criterium {:mvn/version \"0.4.6\"}\n        io.github.rctcwyvrn/blake3 {:mvn/version \"1.3\"}\n        stigmergy/chp {:mvn/version \"0.1.0-SNAPSHOT\"\n                       :exclusions [refactor-nrepl/refactor-nrepl]}}\n :cljd/opts {}\n ;; build/antlr/python3/classes: the generated Python3 parser, produced by\n ;; `clj -M:antlr-gen` (antlr/python3/manifest.edn); absent until generated\n :paths [\"src/clj\" \"src/cljc\" \"src/cljs\" \"src/cljd\"\n         \"build/antlr/python3/classes\" \"build/antlr/php/classes\"\n         \"build/antlr/typescript/classes\"]\n :aliases {:clean {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                   :exec-fn clean/clean}\n           :profile-btree {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                           :extra-deps {persistent-sorted-set/persistent-sorted-set\n                                        {:mvn/version \"0.3.0\"}}\n                           :main-opts [\"-m\" \"btree-bench\"]}\n           :test {:extra-paths [\"test\"]\n                  :extra-deps {io.github.cognitect-labs/test-runner\n                               {:git/tag \"v0.5.1\" :git/sha \"dfb30dd\"}\n                               ;; latte-kernel: the LaTTe proof assistant's kernel,\n                               ;; the differential oracle for the yang.clojure gap\n                               ;; program (pinned; MIT; dependency-free)\n                               latte-kernel/latte-kernel\n                               {:git/url \"https://github.com/latte-central/latte-kernel\"\n                                :git/sha \"86fca0d1d054af005cb37395eae04500d45e6279\"}}\n                  ;; The guest recursion limit allows depth that needs more than the\n                  ;; JVM's platform default thread stack (1024 KB on Linux, 2048 KB on\n                  ;; macOS): under 1 MB the guard loses to a JVM StackOverflowError\n                  ;; before it can raise Python's RecursionError, so the same tests\n                  ;; pass on macOS and fail on Linux. 4 MB is twice the measured\n                  ;; macOS-default minimum, leaving headroom as the prelude grows.\n                  ;; Evidence: docs/agents/known-gaps.md (the recursion rows).\n                  ;; merged stack policy: -Xss64m supersedes the earlier -Xss4m —\n                  ;; deep assertion payloads (the PHPT selection diagnostics and\n                  ;; the conformance compositions) overflow smaller stacks in\n                  ;; clojure.test's printer on shiva (verified green at -Xss64m;\n                  ;; the 4m minimum for the prelude recursion rows still holds)\n                  :jvm-opts [\"-Xss64m\"]\n                  :main-opts [\"-m\" \"cognitect.test-runner\"]\n                  :exec-fn cognitect.test-runner.api/test}\n           :nREPL {:extra-paths [\"test\"]\n                   :extra-deps {nrepl/nrepl {:mvn/version \"1.0.0\"}\n                                cider/cider-nrepl {:mvn/version \"0.58.0\"}}\n                   :main-opts [\"-m\" \"nrepl.cmdline\" \"--middleware\" \"[cider.nrepl/cider-middleware]\"]}\n           :cljs {:extra-paths [\"test\" \"src/dev\" \"build/antlr/python3/js\"]\n                  :extra-deps {thheller/shadow-cljs {:mvn/version \"3.3.5\"}\n                               reagent/reagent {:mvn/version \"2.0.1\"}\n                               com.cognitect/transit-cljs {:mvn/version \"0.8.280\"}}}\n           :dev {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                 :extra-deps {thheller/shadow-cljs {:mvn/version \"3.3.5\"}\n                              cider/cider-nrepl {:mvn/version \"0.58.0\"}\n                              reagent/reagent {:mvn/version \"2.0.1\"}\n                              com.cognitect/transit-cljs {:mvn/version \"0.8.280\"}\n                              ;; benchmark baseline + psset-fixtures\n                              ;; generator only; production uses\n                              ;; dao.data.btree\n                              persistent-sorted-set/persistent-sorted-set\n                              {:mvn/version \"0.3.0\"}}\n                 :jvm-opts [\"-Dconfig=config.clj\"]}\n           ;; The one ClojureDart pin. Compose it with the build aliases:\n           ;; clj -M:clojuredart:cljd ..., clj -M:clojuredart:cljd-yin-repl-build ...\n           :clojuredart {:extra-deps {sonwh98/clojuredart\n                                      {:git/url \"https://github.com/sonwh98/ClojureDart.git\"\n                                       :sha \"7d00ee2aa7fa9d990581e78ebc4a66b93cb70a31\"}\n                                      }}\n           :cljd {:extra-paths [\"test\"]\n                  :main-opts [\"-m\" \"cljd.build\"]\n                  :cljd/opts {:kind :flutter :main datomworld.demo.main}}\n           :server {:extra-deps {http-kit/http-kit {:mvn/version \"2.8.0\"}}\n                    :jvm-opts [\"-Dconfig=config.clj\"]\n                    :main-opts [\"-m\" \"world.server\"]}\n           :clj-yin-repl {:main-opts [\"-m\" \"yin.repl.main\"]}\n           ;; jolt host configuration (docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md\n           ;; §4.2-4.3), run through build/tools/jolt/bin/jolt -Srepro -M:jolt.\n           ;; src/jolt precedes src/cljc so a host override wins; src/clj is\n           ;; listed for audited portable namespaces only (its JVM namespaces\n           ;; are selected away by :jolt reader branches, never loaded). No\n           ;; generated Java parser classes, no JS/Dart roots, and no\n           ;; dependency coordinate is fetched until a jolt-loaded namespace\n           ;; needs one.\n           :jolt {:replace-paths [\"src/jolt\" \"src/cljc\" \"src/clj\"]\n                  :replace-deps {}}\n           :jolt-yin-repl {:main-opts [\"-m\" \"yin.repl.main\"]}\n           ;; composed after :jolt (-M:jolt:jolt-test): the oracle tests and\n           ;; src/dev (src/dev/jolt_test_runner.clj and its sentinel)\n           :jolt-test {:extra-paths [\"test\" \"src/dev\"]}\n           ;; DIAGNOSTIC only (-M:jolt:jolt-diag:jolt-test): a jolt stand-in\n           ;; for the corpus loader dao.jing.cbor-fixtures, whose root must\n           ;; precede test (src/dev/jolt_diag/dao/jing/cbor_fixtures.cljc);\n           ;; jolt puts :extra-paths roots first, in alias order (-Spath)\n           :jolt-diag {:extra-paths [\"src/dev/jolt_diag\"]}\n           :cljs-yin-repl\n           {:extra-paths [\"test\" \"src/dev\"]\n            :extra-deps {thheller/shadow-cljs {:mvn/version \"3.3.5\"}\n                         reagent/reagent {:mvn/version \"2.0.1\"}\n                         com.cognitect/transit-cljs {:mvn/version \"0.8.280\"}}\n            :main-opts [\"-m\" \"yin.repl.node-runner\"]}\n           :fmt {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                 :exec-fn tasks/fix-all}\n           ;; Build-time generation of the grammars-v4 Python3 parser.\n           ;; The too